          "en": "The hardest part of permission systems|isn't the technology — it's the balance.|Too strict —|the agent needs confirmation for every step,|and you might as well write the code yourself.|Too loose —|research shows|that after approving a dozen requests in a row,|users stop reading carefully,|and that's when malicious operations slip through.|So the best practice is context-dependent.|For daily development, use default mode —|most operations need confirmation|but reads are automatic.|When trust is high,|switch to acceptEdits to auto-approve file changes.|In CI environments, use allowlist mode|with only pre-defined commands permitted.",
